module ilsd-criminal-local-rules.local-rules

import core.frcrimp45.{ frcrimp45 }

// The Southern District of Illinois's own periods in a criminal case,
// counted under the criminal rules (FRCrimP 45(a) governs "any local
// rule"). The Local Rules and their Appendices apply in all civil and
// criminal proceedings (SDIL-LR 1.1(b)). The court's criminal rules are
// Appendix A, which the vendored document lists by title only. Appendix A
// is vendored apart (sources/ilsd/ilsd-appendix-a-criminal-sectioned.txt)
// and its two stated periods are encoded below: the response to
// presentence report objections (Cr32.1(b)(1)) and the United States
// Attorney's show cause on a search warrant access request (Cr41.1(a)(2)).
// Its other rules state no period from an event: a defense subpoena
// request describes the information sought (Cr17.1); defense counsel
// responds "as promptly as possible" to a presentence interview notice
// (Cr32.1(a)); search warrant cases are unsealed six months after sealing,
// but no clerk action follows without a request, and a further period of
// inaccessibility is set by a judicial officer up to six months (Cr41.1(a)
// (1), (3)); counsel files a notice of appeal or a declination within the
// time for an appeal, FRAP 4(b)'s period in lit/frcrimp (Cr44.1(a)(1));
// and trial dates follow the Speedy Trial Plan (Cr50.1), which is not
// vendored.
//
// Conditions that are not deadlines from an event stay in notes: criminal
// motions are filed in accordance with orders entered at or after
// arraignment (SDIL-LR 7.1(b)(3)), so the civil response and reply periods
// of SDIL-LR 7.1 do not apply; trial dates follow the District's Plan for
// Prompt Disposition of Criminal Cases (SDIL-LR 16.1(e)), and discovery
// the Standard Order for Pretrial Discovery and Inspection (SDIL-LR
// 26.1(e)(1)), neither of which is vendored; review of a magistrate
// judge's release or detention order is sought by motion filed promptly
// and within the time the magistrate judge designates (SDIL-LR 73.1(f)),
// not a period the rule states; a party producing a controlled substance,
// weapon or child pornography exhibit takes custody of it at the end of
// each day and moves to withdraw it at the end of the proceeding (SDIL-LR
// 79.2(c), (d)). Exhibits are kept by the producing attorneys after trial
// unless the presiding judge orders otherwise (SDIL-LR 79.1(b)).
//
// The earlier S.D. Ill. criminal outline's FRCrimP flow and its decisions
// are FRCrimP, Speedy Trial Act or constitutional authority and belong to
// lit/frcrimp; they are removed here: no reporter text is vendored.
/// A final decision is rendered and no appeal is taken from it (SDIL-LR
/// 79.1(b)(1)).
event FinalDecisionUnappealed()
/// The reviewing court's mandate is filed after an appeal (SDIL-LR
/// 79.1(b)(2)).
event AppellateMandateFiled()

/// SDIL-LR 79.1(b)(1): an exhibit not removed by the attorney who produced
/// it, including an electronic file, is destroyed or otherwise disposed of
/// 90 days after a final decision is rendered if no appeal is taken,
/// unless the presiding judge orders otherwise.
deadline exhibit-removal: 90.days after FinalDecisionUnappealed() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "SDIL-LR 79.1(b)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// SDIL-LR 79.1(b)(2): when an appeal is taken, an exhibit not removed is
/// destroyed or otherwise disposed of within 90 days after the reviewing
/// court's mandate is filed; on appeal the parties make the exhibits
/// available for the record under Seventh Circuit Rule 10 (SDIL-LR
/// 79.1(c)).
deadline exhibit-removal-after-mandate: 90.days after AppellateMandateFiled() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "SDIL-LR 79.1(b)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// Objections to the presentence investigation report are received by a
/// party (SDIL-LR Appendix A, Cr32.1(b)(1); FRCrimP 32(f)).
event PresentenceObjectionsReceived()
/// On a request for access to a search warrant case, the Clerk's Office
/// issues a notice directing the United States Attorney to show cause why
/// the case should remain inaccessible (SDIL-LR Appendix A, Cr41.1(a)(2)).
event SearchWarrantShowCauseNoticeIssued()

/// SDIL-LR Appendix A, Cr32.1(b)(1): objections to the presentence report
/// are filed under FRCrimP 32 (its 14-day period stays with lit/frcrimp);
/// every response to the objections is filed and served on the opposing
/// party and the Probation Office within 7 days of receipt of the
/// objections unless the Court directs otherwise.
deadline presentence-objection-response: 7.days after PresentenceObjectionsReceived() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "SDIL-LR Appendix A, Cr32.1(b)(1)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// SDIL-LR Appendix A, Cr41.1(a)(2): the United States Attorney shows
/// cause, within 14 days of the Clerk's notice, why the search warrant case
/// or a portion of it should remain inaccessible; a judicial officer then
/// decides whether good cause supports a further period of no more than
/// six months.
deadline search-warrant-show-cause: 14.days after SearchWarrantShowCauseNoticeIssued() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "SDIL-LR Appendix A, Cr41.1(a)(2)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}
